// A Pilot string holds the bytes written in it; `??=` is three bytes. The
// emitted C writes the string's bytes into a C string literal as they are,
// and `cc -std=c11` (selfhost/cli/test_emit.pilot, native_build.pilot)
// replaces trigraphs in it before anything else: `??=` becomes `#`, `??(`
// `[`, `??)` `]`. So the emitted C writes each `?` that a `?` follows as
// `\?` (C11 6.4.4.4; selfhost/transpile/c_family.pilot without_trigraphs).
//
// Expected, worked by hand: the strings unchanged -> "a??=b", "x??(y??)",
// and "???=" (three `?`s: the second and third escaped, so no `??=` is
// left for the C compiler) -> "???=".
fn main() {
    print("a??=b");
    print("x??(y??)");
    print("???=");
}
